From: Nyx <mail.me.nyx@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 6 Sep 2026 22:40:00 +0530
Subject: [PATCH 4/4] ASoC: es8328: program the MCLK/LRCK ratio in consumer mode too

When the ES8388 is the clock consumer (SoC I2S is master) the driver left
DACCONTROL2/ADCCONTROL5 at 0. On the AMedia RK3588 board (ES8388 on I2S0,
MCLK 12.288 MHz, 48 kHz) that produced garbled output; the vendor driver
always programs the ratio. Use the same lookup as provider mode whenever
the MCLK is known via set_sysclk().
---
diff --git a/sound/soc/codecs/es8328.c b/sound/soc/codecs/es8328.c
index c0d7ce64b2d..49cb7e0cf95 100644
--- a/sound/soc/codecs/es8328.c
+++ b/sound/soc/codecs/es8328.c
@@ -499,6 +499,20 @@ static int es8328_hw_params(struct snd_pcm_substream *substream,
 			return -EINVAL;
 		}
 		ratio = es8328->mclk_ratios[i];
+	} else if (es8328->sysclk_constraints) {
+		/*
+		 * Consumer (slave) mode with a known MCLK: the ES8388 still
+		 * needs the MCLK/LRCK ratio programmed for its internal
+		 * DAC/ADC filters, otherwise playback is garbled. Look the
+		 * ratio up exactly as in provider mode; fall back to 0
+		 * (auto) if the rate is not in the table.
+		 */
+		for (i = 0; i < es8328->sysclk_constraints->count; i++)
+			if (es8328->sysclk_constraints->list[i] ==
+			    params_rate(params))
+				break;
+		ratio = (i < es8328->sysclk_constraints->count) ?
+			es8328->mclk_ratios[i] : 0;
 	} else {
 		ratio = 0;
 		es8328->mclkdiv2 = 0;
